{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Have I conceived, etc.</b> He spoke in this order, and it may be explained according to the words of the Sages, of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 19b), that whoever teaches the son of his friend Torah is as though he gave birth to him. They also said, of blessed memory (Tikkunei Zohar 70), that all the souls of the people of Israel of the wilderness generation were branches of the soul of Moses our teacher, and he was a father to them. Therefore he did not say \"am I the father of this people\" or \"if they are my sons,\" for so it is in truth — that he was a father to their souls, and they are called his sons also on account of his having taught them Torah. Rather, <b>have I conceived</b> — meaning, he spoke against the aspect of their being his branches: what of it? Since no man among them was born from his body, he is not obligated in their care. And against their being called his sons on account of his teaching them Torah, he said, <b>did I give birth to him</b> — for specifically a son who comes by way of birth [obligates], not one who comes through the intermediary of Torah learning." "<b>Further,</b> he intended by the doubling of <b>conceived / and gave birth to him</b> to say that the burden of the people upon him is like the burden of a father and of a mother together — like a son whose mother gave birth to him and died, so that the father must perform the role of father and of mother. Or perhaps it also alludes to two periods during which the father is obligated to bear the burden of the son: one is a matter of Torah law, which is until the son is six or seven years old (Ketuvot 65b), and the second is by rabbinic decree, until thirteen years of age (Ketuvot 49b). And even though the Sages had not yet instituted this additional period, it is a matter grasped by human intellect, and all the more so by Moses, who was a prophet. And you will find that they said, of blessed memory (Yoma 28b), that our father Abraham fulfilled even *eruvei tavshilin* even though they had not yet been instituted; likewise did the prophet of HaShem know of the enactments of the Sages. And he said concerning the two periods: <b>Have I conceived</b> — corresponding to the Torah obligation; <b>did I give birth to him</b> — corresponding to the obligation of rabbinic decree." "And the meaning of the letter *heh* in *ha'Anokhi* is a *heh* of concession, meaning that Moses concedes to be obligated in what a father is obligated toward a son, and even an obligation of rabbinic decree; yet even so, behold — these are great men, beyond the fixed age. Why do You say to me, ״Carry him in your bosom,״ for a man grown in years? Were it that they are my sons — I would be exempt from them. And he returns and further argues: were it that they are judged by the law of a minor, on account of their being deprived of [proper] grasp of sustenance so that their legal status is as minors — even so, he is not obligated to do what they ask of him, to give them meat. And that is what he said, <b>From where do I have meat</b> — meaning, granted, if they were asking for necessary sustenance, he would be obligated by the law of a father; but they are asking for extras. Furthermore, even though they are extras, if it were upon a father to indulge his son according to his ability — [he would], but from where do I have, etc.?"], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:07:44.459973854Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}